# Input
```ts
/* should generate diagnostics */
const a = new Array<number>(5).map((item) => item);
const b = (new Array(5) as number[]).map((item) => item);
const c = new Array(5)!.map((item) => item);

```

# Diagnostics
```
invalid.ts:2:32 lint/nursery/noUninvokedArrayCallback ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  × The callback passed to map() is never called.
  
    1 │ /* should generate diagnostics */
  > 2 │ const a = new Array<number>(5).map((item) => item);
      │                                ^^^
    3 │ const b = (new Array(5) as number[]).map((item) => item);
    4 │ const c = new Array(5)!.map((item) => item);
  
  i This array only has empty slots.
  
    1 │ /* should generate diagnostics */
  > 2 │ const a = new Array<number>(5).map((item) => item);
      │           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    3 │ const b = (new Array(5) as number[]).map((item) => item);
    4 │ const c = new Array(5)!.map((item) => item);
  
  i Calling Array with a single number creates an array with that many empty slots instead of undefined values. map() doesn't call its callback for empty slots.
  
  i Fill the array first, for example with new Array(length).fill(), or create it with Array.from({ length }, callback).
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

```

